WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:01.084
My plan had

00:00:01.084 --> 00:00:05.005
originally been to enlist in the Army
and then use that to pay for college.

00:00:05.046 --> 00:00:09.300
Once I found out about QuestBridge
and the generous financial aid,

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:12.804
the colleges like Yale have,
that sort of changed my plans.

00:00:13.138 --> 00:00:16.599
I found out about QuestBridge
actually through a pamphlet in my guidance

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:17.851
counselors office.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:21.521
I found out about QuestBridge
through my AP English language teacher.

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.773
I found out about QuestBridge
through my older brother.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.568
We both knew that if we were going
to go to college out of state,

00:00:26.651 --> 00:00:29.154
we were going to have to find scholarships
and grants to do that.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:33.867
I was invited to the QuestBridge Conference
here at Yale.

00:00:33.908 --> 00:00:37.078
We have a mini college fair
where I really got to meet

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:39.414
a lot of different colleges
and including Yale.

00:00:39.664 --> 00:00:40.540
When you hear, you know,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.711
you get to go college for free, you get over
$300,000 worth of scholarships.

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:46.546
Doesn't seem plausible.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.799
I was like,
Is this real, is this legit?

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.177
I just went ahead and applied
because it was free.

00:00:52.802 --> 00:00:56.556
I decided I would do the National College
Match because it just seemed

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:59.768
like a really good way
for me to showcase who I am.

00:01:01.186 --> 00:01:02.645
I focused on QuestBridge

00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:06.941
because the profile of students
that they described was exactly who I was.

00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:10.278
My test scores, my EFC, my background.

00:01:10.612 --> 00:01:12.614
It felt like the perfect fit for me.

00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:17.202
I really found that it was nice
to just have it established

00:01:17.202 --> 00:01:20.413
through applying through QuestBridge
that I was first generation low income.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.790
I didn't have to talk about that.

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.125
All I had to do is talk about myself
and tell my story.

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:27.295
I wasn't trying to fit
into a mold of different

00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:30.131
activities or things that weren't
even accessible at my high school.

00:01:30.673 --> 00:01:33.593
It allowed me to present my story
and who I am

00:01:33.885 --> 00:01:36.638
more than just
what I've done academically.

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:40.600
Knowing that if I matched,
I would have a full financial aid

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.187
package was like,
oh, this is really within my reach.

00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:45.438
Like, they want people like me.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:47.774
That's why they're offering this.

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:51.444
Now, me as an admissions officer
on the other side of the application,

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:56.533
it really helps us to read and understand
that story to get a fuller context

00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:59.953
of who this applicant is
and how they might fit in on our campus.

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:03.164
When my community

00:02:03.164 --> 00:02:06.709
and my family found out that I had matched,
they freaked out.

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:09.170
I didn't expect to get in.

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:11.297
It was just a big celebration moment.

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:12.674
It was preparing for a future

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:15.051
that I never thought
that I was going to be able to have.

00:02:15.051 --> 00:02:17.053
The day I was accepted to Yale.

00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:18.972
I was like, Wait, no way, This is a prank.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:20.140
And then I was like, Wait, no, it's not.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.018
I'm running through the house and I'm like, Mom, Mom
you know, look, but

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:26.062
wait a minute,
because I need to check the financial aid

00:02:26.062 --> 00:02:29.399
because if I don't have good aid,
I'm not going to be able to go.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:32.861
And I was like, they're literally like
going to pay for everything.

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:37.073
I had actually never flown by myself
and I was really nervous

00:02:37.073 --> 00:02:40.869
to get on a plane
with everything I owned and go to college.

00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:43.955
You kind of feel like, okay, I'm
going to be the only one who's

00:02:43.955 --> 00:02:46.040
the first person
to go to college in their family,

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.752
the only one from a small town, you know,
the only one from from this background.

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:51.254
But when you get here, you see that

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:54.591
there's thousands of other people
with the same experience, you know.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:58.678
The Quest Scholars Network at Yale
has truly become my family.

00:02:58.928 --> 00:03:01.431
I kind of get emotional
just talking about the people

00:03:01.431 --> 00:03:03.766
that I've met
through QuestBridge on campus.

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:08.771
I would say the first generation
college student community is very vibrant

00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:10.273
and diverse.

00:03:10.273 --> 00:03:13.193
Just knowing that everyone has gone
through similar things as me

00:03:13.484 --> 00:03:14.861
is comforting.

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:19.240
It is a shared identity that, you know,
hundreds of students come to campus having

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:23.119
and the Quest Scholars Network
is a place for them to share in that,

00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:26.998
to come together as a community and sort
of build a home home within Yale.

00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:29.042
It's been very valuable in terms

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:32.086
of finding a community
where I feel at home and not out of place.

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:36.841
I think my own preconceived
notions were absolutely inaccurate about,

00:03:36.841 --> 00:03:37.217
you know,

00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:41.471
I felt like I really found a place that
I fit in at, a sort of home away from home.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.432
I've never felt,
you know, like I didn't belong here.

00:03:45.016 --> 00:03:47.518
Sometimes the imposter
syndrome really hits me,

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:49.687
but then I'm like, I got here, too.

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:51.856
Like, in my own way,
it was a different route.

00:03:51.856 --> 00:03:54.359
But I got here, and I deserve to be here.

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:57.070
It's going to be hard
because you're doing this by yourself.

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:59.113
But these schools want you.

00:03:59.113 --> 00:04:01.449
You deserve to be at Yale.

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:02.533
You can do it.

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:04.577
And you have to have your own cheerleader.

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:09.082
And it is hard, but it's worth it
because Yale is the best thing

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:09.874
that's ever happened to me.

